-"Define promotion semantics, including when a proof produces a promotable
-top-level conclusion" is left unchecked deliberately: `promote_theorem`
-and `_top_level_derived_formulas` (`ProofLogic.py`) already implement and
-partially document this (a promotable conclusion is the last top-level
-formula justified by a `'rule'`/`'rule_below'`/`'rule_hybrid'` tag,
-explicitly excluding premises and declarations), predating this phase's
-work -- nothing here changed or added to that logic, so checking this box
-would overclaim. Revisit as its own task: read that logic critically for
-edge cases (a proof that derives nothing at the top level but only inside
-a subproof; a proof whose intended theorem isn't its literal final line,
-which `promote_theorem`'s explicit `conclusion=` parameter already has an
-escape hatch for) and decide whether it needs anything beyond what's
