+The two edge cases flagged above were both checked empirically, not just
+reasoned about, with different outcomes:
+
+- A proof deriving *more than one* top-level formula, whose `### then
+  ...` line names which one is the real theorem, is a genuine bug that
+  was fixed: `promote_theorem`'s default (the literal last top-level
+  derived formula) silently promoted the *wrong* conclusion whenever the
+  stated one wasn't the last line, even though `run_multi_proof_file`
+  already had `case.stated_conclusion` in hand and used it for the
+  `conclusion_is_derived` check immediately above -- it just never
+  passed that value through to `promote_theorem` itself. Fixed by doing
+  exactly that (`conclusion=case.stated_conclusion`); safe unconditionally
+  because reaching that call already means `conclusion_is_derived`
+  passed, so a non-`None` stated conclusion is always something the
+  proof actually derived. `test_promotion_honors_a_stated_conclusion_
+  that_is_not_the_final_line` (`pytest_tests/test_theorem_promotion_and_
+  set_theory.py`) reproduces the exact scenario that surfaced this and
+  was confirmed to fail without the fix.
+- A proof that validates while deriving *nothing* at its own top level
+  (all its real content sitting inside a standalone subproof block
+  nothing afterward ever cites) is *not* a gap: `promote_theorem` already
+  refuses with a clear, honest message, which is the correct behavior --
+  such a proof genuinely proves nothing at its own top level, unlike the
+  case above, where something true was silently attributed to the wrong
+  formula. Locked in as a regression guard, not left as an assumption,
+  via `test_promote_theorem_refuses_a_proof_whose_only_content_is_an_
+  uncited_standalone_subproof` (same file), so a future change to
+  `_top_level_derived_formulas` that started recursing into subproofs --
+  which would reopen exactly the silent-wrong-conclusion risk the first
+  bug above was -- would have to consciously break this test rather than
+  drift into it unnoticed.
+
+Phase 3 is now complete.
 
